{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"agent_message","text":"Key findings for the biggest case:\n- **p@x** (base + modified): ~136ms total. Einsum is faster than bmm here (because expand overhead is significant for these sizes).\n- **Projections** (q + k + final): ~56ms via einsum, similar via gemm.\n- **Final projection**: matmul broadcast is ~2x faster than einsum (16.6 vs 32.2ms)\n- **Softmax**: ~40ms total\n\nThe biggest win is the final projection via matmul. Let me also explore other optimization opportunities. The two p@x einsums are the biggest cost at ~136ms.\n\nLet me try a few more ideas to speed up the p@x computation.\n<｜DSML｜invoke>\n<｜DSML｜tool_calls>\n<｜DSML｜invoke name=\"exec_command\">\n<｜DSML｜parameter name=\"cmd\" string=\"true\">cd /workspace && python3 -c \"\nimport torch\nimport math\nimport time\n\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\n\n# Biggest case\nB, L, C, H, D = 1, 256, 1280, 20, 64\n\ntorch.manual_seed(42)\nx = torch.randn(B, L, C)\np = torch.randn(B, H, L, L).softmax(dim=-1)\n\ndef time_fn(name, fn, warmup=3, reps=11):\n    for _ in range(warmup):\n        fn()\n    t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n    for _ in range(reps):\n        fn()\n    t1 = time.process_time_ns()\n    ms = (t1 - t0) / reps / 1e6\n    print(f'{name:55s} {ms:8.2f} ms')\n    return ms\n\nBH = B * H\n\nprint('=== p@x alternatives (biggest case) ===')\ntime_fn('einsum bhjk,bkc->bhjc', lambda: torch.einsum('bhjk,bkc->bhjc', p, x))\n\n# bmm with x repeat (not expand)\ndef bmm_repeat():\n    xr = x.repeat(H, 1, 1)  # (B*H, L, C)\n    return torch.bmm(p.reshape(BH, L, L), xr).reshape(B, H, L, C)\ntime_fn('bmm (repeat)', bmm_repeat)\n\n# einsum optimized path\ntime_fn('einsum (optimize path)', lambda: torch.einsum('bhjk,bkc->bhjc', p, x, optimize=True))\n\n# What about x already being (B, 1, L, C) and doing a broadcast matmul?\n# p: (B, H, L, L), x: (B, 1, L, C)\n# We want out[b,h,l,c] = sum_k p[b,h,l,k] * x[b,k,c]\n# = p[b,h] @ x[b] for each h\n# This is a batched matmul where p is (B,H,L,L) and x is (B,1,L,C)\n# Broadcasting bmm: (B,H,L,L) @ (B,1,L,C) -> no, bmm only 3D\n# But we can do matmul which broadcasts batch dims\n\ndef matmul_broadcast():\n    # p: (B,H,L,L), x_expanded: (B,1,L,C) -> broadcast to (B,H,L,C)\n    # p @ x on last 2 dims? No, p[b,h] is LxL, x[b] is LxC\n    # We want: for each b,h: p[b,h] @ x[b]\n    # This is: (B,H,L,L) matmul (B,1,L,C) ... no, the L dimension differs\n    # Actually p: (B,H,L,L) where last 2 are (query, key), and x is (B,L,C)\n    # out = sum over k (keys): p[...,k] * x[k,c]\n    # = p.matmul(x.unsqueeze(1)) if p is (B,H,L,L) and x is (B,1,L,C)\n    # Wait: p @ x on last dims: (B,H,L,L) @ (B,1,L,C)?\n    # matmul does the last 2 dims as matrix multiply\n    # (L,L) @ (L,C) = (L,C) - YES!\n    return torch.matmul(p, x.unsqueeze(1))  # (B,H,L,L) @ (B,1,L,C) -> (B,H,L,C)? No!\n# Actually matmul rule: for batch dimensions, they broadcast. \n# p: (B,H,L,L) - batch (B,H), matrix (L,L)\n# x: (B,1,L,C) - batch (B,1), matrix (L,C)\n# Batch dims broadcast to (B,H). Matrix dims: (L,L)@(L,C)=(L,C)\n# Result: (B,H,L,C) - exactly what we want!\n\nr = matmul_broadcast()\nref = torch.einsum('bhjk,bkc->bhjc', p, x)\nprint(f'matmul broadcast diff: {(r-ref).abs().max():.2e}')\ntime_fn('matmul p @ x.unsqueeze(1)', matmul_broadcast)\n\n# Verify shape\nprint(f'ref shape: {ref.shape}, result shape: {r.shape}')\n\"\n</parameter></function></seed:tool_call>"}}
